(see Algebra, Definition \\ref{algebra-definition-formally-etale})\nif for every commutative solid diagram\n$$\n\\xymatrix{\nA \\ar[r] \\ar@{-->}[rd] & B/I \\\\\nR \\ar[r] \\ar[u] & B \\ar[u]\n}\n$$\nwhere $I \\subset B$ is an ideal of square zero, there exists\nexactly one dotted arrow which makes the diagram commute. This motivates\nthe following analogue for morphisms of schemes.", "section_start_line": 1491, "section_title": "Formally \\'etale morphisms", "source_filename": "more-morphisms.tex", "source_label": "lemma-formally-etale-not-affine", "source_revision": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_statement_line": 1527, "source_url": "https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-morphisms.tex#L1527", "statement_tex": "\\label{lemma-formally-etale-not-affine}\nIf $f : X \\to S$ is a formally \\'etale morphism, then given\nany solid commutative diagram\n$$\n\\xymatrix{\nX \\ar[d]_f & T \\ar[d]^i \\ar[l] \\\\\nS & T' \\ar[l] \\ar@{-->}[lu]\n}\n$$\nwhere $T \\subset T'$ is a first order thickening of schemes over $S$\nthere exists exactly one dotted arrow making the diagram commute.\nIn other words, in\nDefinition \\ref{definition-formally-etale}\nthe condition that $T$ be affine may be dropped.", "statement_truncated": false}
